{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nA recurring defect shape — a check whose PASS/absent/empty result is indistinguishable from the\ncheck never having run — appeared nine times in one session (2026-08-20/21). Code review has no\ndetection language for it: reviewers confirm an assertion is correct without asking *what else\nproduces this same negative result?* A fail-open check is silent, so it accumulates green\nhistory cited as evidence of health. Per this ticket's refinement comment, the mirror is\nincluded: a broken instrument can also produce a confident FAILING result for a reason other\nthan the condition, so the language covers \"any definite result\", not just PASS.\n\n**OPERATOR RULING (ticket comment, 2026-08-21): do NOT create a new criterion.** Add the\nfail-open / producer-liveness detection language to the EXISTING `tests` criterion (test-oracle\npopulation) and `error-handling` criterion (shipped-code population). Escalate to a new\ncriterion only if calibration proves the language cannot fire from within those hosts.\n\n### The nine instances (calibration corpus; full narratives in this ticket's description\nhistory and comments)\n\n`tests` host (test-oracle): (1) `sound-bumpkinly-thrush` — absence asserted on a child's output;\na signal-killed child writes nothing, so never-ran reports GREEN; (2) `abusive-pebbly-sakimonkey`\n— a convergence test RE-DERIVED the send path's dedup key instead of driving the send path;\n(3) a harness grepped `\"^FAILED\"` against COLORIZED pytest output — reported \"0 failed\"\nregardless; (4) an orphaned sweep's truncated log and a clean log produce the SAME empty grep.\n\n`error-handling` host (shipped code): (5) `obvious-saline-gerbil` — pass health never checked\nthe returncode; (6) `defiable-distortive-pinniped` — `gen_env_registry` failed OPEN on a helper\nmissing from `KNOWN_ENV_HELPERS` (absent was invisible, not an error); (7)\n`blocky-matricidal-squid` — the `os.environ` half, same posture; (8) a `pgrep -f \"make test\"`\nmonitor matched its own shell and waited for itself; (9) a `pgrep -f \"review-plan <alias>\"`\nwaiter matched its own process.\n\n## Approach\n\nEach host criterion's rubric already has a home (confirmed by reading the tree):\n\n- `tests`: the full rubric lives in the overlay prompt\n  `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-tests.md`. Add a **fail-open oracle** antipattern: an\n  ABSENCE assertion (`not in`, `assert not`, `== \"\"`, empty-collection) derived from a\n  subprocess/file/network/captured output, or a grep/regex over another tool's output used as a\n  pass/fail signal, with NO liveness check on the producer (exit status, non-empty capture, an\n  asserted presence). Litmus (the ticket's reviewer prompt, broadened per the refinement\n  comment): *name every world in which the check produces its definite result — pass or fail;\n  if \"the producer never ran / emitted nothing\" is among them, the check cannot distinguish its\n  verdict from its own failure.* Qualify FP guard (iii) (\"greps of COMMAND OUTPUT are valid\") to\n  require established producer liveness, and add the matching FP guard for absence assertions\n  preceded by a liveness assertion.\n- `error-handling` is a BASE-REVIEWER dimension (per its criteria_routing.json `_comment`), so\n  its language goes in `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-base.md` §\"Surface grounded\n  findings\": a short fail-open paragraph naming the shipped-code triggers — a silent\n  lookup/membership MISS path, a subprocess check that never reads the returncode, a\n  `pgrep`/`pkill` pattern that can match the matching process itself, any best-effort check\n  whose failure is invisible — with the same litmus and the uniform remediation (assert the\n  producer RAN before trusting any absence derived from it).\n- Registry record: extend the `_comment` of the `tests` and `error-handling` entries in\n  `src/rebar/llm/code_review/criteria_routing.json` (this ticket + trigger list + placement\n  ruling). No posture/threshold/blocking values change.\n- Calibration corpus: add the nine instances as seeded-diff recall cases (pre-fix,\n  `expect: finding`) each paired with a post-fix no-fire case (`expect: pass`) — instances 1–4\n  in `src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/code-review-tests.eval.yaml`, 5–9 in\n  `src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/code-review-base.eval.yaml`, dso-corpus house style.\n- Docs: code-review criteria have no per-criterion `rebar explain <id>` section (`_explain` in\n  `src/rebar/_cli/_llm_commands.py` covers plan-review criteria + guides only); the code-review\n  author surface is the packaged guide behind `rebar explain review`\n  (`src/rebar/_guides/passing-code-review.md`). Default taken (recorded as a ticket comment):\n  document the pattern there with two worked examples (instances 1 and 6), rather than building\n  a new explain surface for code-review criterion ids.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-tests.md` — fail-open oracle antipattern + FP guard.\n- `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-base.md` — error-handling fail-open paragraph.\n- `src/rebar/llm/code_review/criteria_routing.json` — `_comment` provenance only.\n- `src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/code-review-tests.eval.yaml` — 4 recall + 4 no-fire cases.\n- `src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/code-review-base.eval.yaml` — 5 recall + 5 no-fire cases.\n- `src/rebar/_guides/passing-code-review.md` — the pattern + two worked examples.\n\n## Testing\n\n- Offline (no model): `pytest tests/unit/test_code_review_evals.py` — both extended specs must\n  stay strict-clean (parse, `validate_eval_spec(strict=True)`, registered scorers, balanced\n  fire/no-fire, every case carries a `diff`).\n- Live calibration replay (bedrock via `REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE`): run each of the 18 new cases\n  through its Pass-1 prompt via `rebar.llm.evals.eval_solver.run_case` (`code-review-tests` for\n  1–4, `code-review-base` for 5–9); a pre-fix case must yield ≥1 finding, a post-fix case none\n  attributable to the new language. Record per-case results as a ticket comment.\n- FP posture: both hosts are ALREADY blocking (`tests` @0.54, `error-handling` @0.50), so no\n  advisory ramp applies; the FP measurement is (a) the 9 post-fix no-fire cases and (b) the\n  specs' standing no-fire families staying silent in the same replay. Record both.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `code-review-tests.md` names the fail-open oracle antipattern with the producer-liveness\n      litmus and matching FP guard; checked by\n      `rg \"fail-open\" src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-tests.md`.\n- [ ] `code-review-base.md` carries the error-handling fail-open language (silent lookup miss,\n      unchecked returncode, pgrep self-match, invisible best-effort failure); checked by\n      `rg \"fail-open\" src/rebar/llm/reviewers/code-review-base.md`.\n- [ ] The `tests` and `error-handling` `_comment` fields in\n      `src/rebar/llm/code_review/criteria_routing.json` record the triggers + this ticket; no\n      routing VALUE changes (checked by diff).\n- [ ] Calibration: all 9 pre-fix cases fire and all 9 post-fix cases are silent in the live\n      replay; per-case results recorded as a ticket comment.\n- [ ] FP posture recorded in the same comment (hosts already blocking; standing no-fire\n      families silent).\n- [ ] `rebar explain review` documents the pattern with ≥2 worked examples; checked by\n      `rebar explain review | rg -c \"fail-open\"` ≥ 1 plus reading the examples.\n- [ ] Eval specs strict-clean offline; checked by `pytest tests/unit/test_code_review_evals.py`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787353019213192001,"uuid":"c434940d-4363-4bcc-bc9b-735c28cc37ac"}